of 29, which is slightly higher than the S&P 500's P/E ratio of 25.\"\n- **v6-2-7** (stored attribution: Nvidia): Nvidia reported Q2 FY2027 operating income of $63.7 billion, up 124% year over year.\n  - Passage: \"Operating income surged 124% YoY to $63.7 billion.\"\n- **v6-2-8** (stored attribution: Wall Street analysts): The author says Wall Street expects Nvidia revenue growth of 91% in FY2027 and 66% in FY2028.\n  - Passage: \"Wall Street expects the company to grow revenue by 91% this year and 66% next year.\"\n- **v6-2-9** (stored attribution: Keithen Drury): The author said Broadcom and its AI hyperscaler clients are designing workload-specific AI computing units that perform AI computing at lower cost and more effectively than Nvidia's GPUs, threatening Nvidia's market share.\n  - Passage: \"There are also workload-specific AI computing units being designed by Broadcom ( AVGO +2.83% ) and its AI hyperscaler clients that perform AI computing at lower cost and more effectively than Nvidia's or AMD's GPUs, threatening Nvidia's market share.\"\n- **v6-2-10** (stored attribution: TSMC): TSMC announced an additional $100 billion investment in its Arizona production facilities during Q2.\n  - Passage: \"Furthermore, TSMC announced an additional $100 billion investment in its Arizona production facilities during Q2, so clearly it sees more demand coming that requires increased production capacity.\"\n- **v6-2-11** (stored attribution: Nvidia): Nvidia reported fiscal Q2 2027 net income of $59.7 billion, up 126% year over year.\n  - Passage: \"Net income rose 126% year over year, to $59.7 billion.\"\n- **v6-2-12** (stored attribution: IonQ and Nvidia): IonQ announced a partnership with Nvidia to deploy IonQ's quantum computer at Nvidia's Accelerated Quantum Research Center, aligning IonQ's trapped-ion hardware with Nvidia's CUDA-Q platform.\n  - Passage: \"IonQ (IONQ) shares inched higher on Sept. 23 after announcing a landmark partnership with Nvidia (NVDA) to deploy its quantum computer at the AI titan's Accelerated Quantum Research Center.\nThe deal aligns IonQ's trapped-ion hardware with Nvidia's CUDA-Q platform, advancing hybrid classical-quantum supercomputing.\"\n- **v6-2-13** (stored attribution: Nvidia): Nvidia's Q3 FY2027 revenue forecast assumes zero Data Center compute revenue from China.\n  - Passage: \"Importantly, the forecast assumes no Data Center compute revenue from China.\"\n- **v6-2-14** (stored attribution: Wall Street analysts): Wall Street's consensus mean price target for Nvidia was about $326.\n  - Passage: \"the mean price target of about $326\"\n- **v6-2-15** (stored attribution: Nvidia): Nvidia projected that global annual data-center capital expenditures would reach $3 trillion to $4 trillion by 2030.\n  - Passage: \"Ultimately, Nvidia sees global annual data center capital expenditures increasing to $3 trillion to $4 trillion by 2030.\"\n- **v6-2-16** (stored attribution: Eric Xu, Huawei chairman): Huawei chairman Eric Xu said Huawei may be unable to make enough chips to meet domestic demand.\n  - Passage: \"Chairman Eric Xu acknowledged that Huawei may not be able to make enough chips to meet domestic demand\"\n- **v6-2-17** (stored attribution: TrendForce): TrendForce estimated that TSMC generated 72.5% of semiconductor revenue in the relevant space during the second quarter.\n  - Passage: \"During the second quarter, TrendForce estimates that TSMC generated 72.5% of all semiconductor revenue in the space.\"\n- **v6-2-18** (stored attribution: NVIDIA): NVIDIA reported Q2 FY2027 Data Center revenue of $89.023 billion.\n  - Passage: \"Data Center revenue hit $89.023 billion.\"\n- **v6-2-19** (stored attribution: Nvidia): Nvidia reported Q2 FY2027 revenue of $96.2 billion, up 106% year over year and 18% sequentially.\n  - Passage: \"Revenue reached a record $96.2 billion, up 106% year-over-year (YoY) from $46.7 billion, and increased 18% sequentially.\"\n- **v6-2-20** (stored attribution: NVIDIA): NVIDIA is committing $1 billion to Nscale's $3.1 billion convertible financing; the article reports no completion date.\n  - Passage: \"Yet NVIDIA Corporation (NASDAQ: NVDA ) is committing $1 billion to a $3.1 billion convertible financing, while Microsoft Corporation (NASDAQ: MSFT ) is expected to become one of Nscale's most important customers.\"\n- **v6-2-21** (stored attribution: fool.com analyst): The author estimates Nvidia trades at less than 15 times next year's earnings, and judges that the valuation prices in almost no growth.\n  - Passage: \"At less than 15 times next year's earnings, Nvidia's stock has almost no growth priced into it today.\"\n- **v6-2-22** (stored attribution: Colette Kress, Nvidia CFO): Nvidia CFO Colette Kress expected Nvidia revenue to grow approximately 70% in FY2028.\n  - Passage: \"We expect to grow revenue by approximately 70% in fiscal 2028,\"\n- **v6-2-23** (stored attribution: Apple): Apple is pitching its newest high-end Macs to enterprise customers as an alternative to cloud AI infrastructure, potentially competing with demand for Nvidia data-center AI.\n  - Passage: \"Apple ( NASDAQ:AAPL ) is making an unusually aggressive push into enterprise artificial intelligence , pitching its newest high-end Macs as an alternative to the expensive cloud infrastructure that has helped Nvidia ( NASDAQ:NVDA ) and Microsoft ( NASDAQ:MSFT ) dominate the AI boom.\"\n- **v6-2-24** (stored attribution: NVIDIA): NVIDIA's preliminary expectation was for total revenue growth of approximately 70% in FY2028.\n  - Passage: \"a preliminary fiscal 2028 growth expectation of approximately 70%\"\n- **v6-2-25** (stored attribution: Nvidia): Nvidia estimates global data-center capital expenditures will reach $3 trillion to $4 trillion by 2030.\n  - Passage: \"Nvidia estimates that global data center capital expenditures will rise to between $3 trillion and $4 trillion by 2030.\"\n- **v6-2-26** (stored attribution: NVIDIA): NVIDIA said it is working to increase supply to meet customer demand.\n  - Passage: \"Nvidia says it's working to increase supply to meet customer demand.\"\n- **v6-2-27** (stored attribution: Calcalist): Calcalist reported that a new Nvidia AI research group focused on medical and scientific applications had begun operating in Israel.\n  - Passage: \"A new Nvidia research group focused on using artificial intelligence to accelerate medical and scientific breakthroughs has begun operating in Israel, Calcalist has learned.\"\n- **v6-2-28** (stored attribution: Daniel Sparks): The author calculated that Nvidia shares at $227.38 traded at about 15 times expected FY2028 earnings.\n  - Passage: \"At Monday's close of $227.38, shares trade at about 15 times what the company is expected to earn in fiscal 2028, the year that outlook applies to.\"\n- **v6-2-29** (stored attribution: Nvidia): Nvidia’s website listed several research positions related to the new group at its Tel Aviv offices.\n  - Passage: \"The company’s website already lists several research positions in fields related to the group at its Tel Aviv offices.\"\n- **v6-2-30** (stored attribution: Nvidia): Nvidia reported Q2 FY2027 non-GAAP net income of $54 billion, up 118% year over year, and adjusted EPS of $2.22, up 120%.\n  - Passage: \"The company's profitability expanded even faster. On a non-GAAP basis, net income climbed 118% to $54 billion, versus $24.8 billion in Q2 fiscal 2026, while adjusted EPS jumped 120% to $2.22, exceeding expectations.\"\n- **v6-2-31** (stored attribution: Nvidia): Nvidia guided Q3 FY2027 revenue to $108 billion, plus or minus 2%.\n  - Passage: \"For Q3 fiscal 2027, the company expects revenue of $108 billion, plus or minus 2%.\"\n- **v6-2-32** (stored attribution: Nvidia): Nvidia reported Q2 FY2027 Data Center revenue of $89 billion, up 117% year over year.\n  - Passage: \"Nvidia's Data Center segment remained the primary growth engine, generating $89 billion in revenue, up 117% YoY from the prior-year quarter and representing the majority of total company revenue.\"\n- **v6-2-33** (stored attribution: Skild, Nvidia and Foxconn): Skild, Nvidia and Foxconn recently began deploying Skild Brain on dual-arm robots at an Nvidia factory in Houston to assemble Nvidia Blackwell GPU systems.\n  - Passage: \"The technology is already being deployed at an Nvidia factory in Houston, where Skild, Nvidia and contract electronics manufacturing giant Foxconn recently began using the Skild Brain omni-bodied foundation model on dual-arm robots to assemble Nvidia Blackwell GPU systems.\"\n- **v6-2-34** (stored attribution: NVIDIA): NVIDIA reported non-GAAP gross margin of 75% in fiscal Q2 2027, versus 72.5% a year earlier.\n  - Passage: \"NVIDIA's non-GAAP gross margin increased to 75% in the fiscal second quarter compared with 72.5% a year ago.\"\n- **v6-2-35** (stored attribution: fool.com analyst): Nvidia's receivables increased from $38.5 billion in January to $63.1 billion at the end of July.\n  - Passage: \"A big part of that is the company's receivables rising from $38.5 billion in January to $63.1 billion at the end of July.\"\n- **v6-2-36** (stored attribution: AMD): AMD reported second-quarter 2026 Data Center segment revenue of $6.7 billion, up more than 100% year over year.\n  - Passage: \"more than doubled year over year, totaling $6.7 billion (58% of the company's revenue).\"\n- **v6-2-37** (stored attribution: Nvidia): Nvidia reported Q2 FY2027 non-GAAP gross margin of 75%, compared with 72.5% in Q2 FY2026.\n  - Passage: \"Non-GAAP gross margin rose to 75% from 72.5%.\"\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
